 "why": "ERCOT's West hub had 308 hours with a negative mean real-time price in 2019 and 638 in 2025 (3.5 and 7.3 percent of the year's held hours); Houston had 46 and 142. West's worst year was 2022 with 1,014 hours; North had 224 in 2025 and South 235. Counted by 15-minute interval West's 2025 figure is 2,650 intervals. SPP's North hub, held from 2024-09-01, had 1,044 negative hours in 2025 (11.9 percent of 8,760 held hours). So far in 2026: West 936 hours, Houston 117, of 7,175 held. A negative hour is wind or solar paid to run (tax credits, contracts) behind a line that cannot carry it east; the hubs name where that happens. The count says how often, not what it cost anyone.",
 "footnote": "Data: real-time settlement point prices, 15-minute: ERCOT West: HB_WEST, 2018-01-01 to 2026-10-27 (274,172 intervals, America/Chicago); ERCOT Houston: HB_HOUSTON, 2018-01-01 to 2026-10-27 (274,172 intervals, America/Chicago); ERCOT North: HB_NORTH, 2018-01-01 to 2026-10-27 (274,172 intervals, America/Chicago); ERCOT South: HB_SOUTH, 2018-01-01 to 2026-10-27 (274,172 intervals, America/Chicago); SPP North: SPPNORTH_HUB, 2024-09-01 to 2026-10-26 (74,688 intervals, America/Chicago). An hour is the local clock hour; its price is the mean of its held intervals; it counts as negative when that mean is below 0 USD/MWh (the threshold input); the share is of the year's held hours; the interval count is of intervals below the same threshold. The ERCOT hubs' history runs from 2015 (ercot_all_hub_prices_history, ERCOT NP6-785-ER and NP6-905-CD) and the live table from 2026-08-26; SPP North from 2024-09-01 (iso_hub_prices_history, SPP's RTBM settlement prices). A year a hub does not cover is not held and not filled. No cap, no exclusion.",
